{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"gold-distinct-lacewing","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# A tracker re-clone silently re-identifies the environment, invalidating every attestation\n\n`.env-id` is git-ignored local state (tracker `.gitignore` lines 1, 13, 14 cover `.env-id`,\n`.opcert-key`, `.opcert-key.pub`) that is STAMPED INTO EVERY EVENT and is the `principal` of\nevery op-cert attestation. Re-cloning the tracker without carrying it over mints a NEW\nenvironment identity, and every attestation signed by the old one becomes unverifiable.\n\n## Observed\nThe `.tickets-tracker` was converted to a standalone clone on 2026-08-09. `.env-id` was\nregenerated at 20:39 (`b8a354e0-…`, replacing `ade0f2ef-…`) and a new `.opcert-key` at 20:50.\nStore scan: 2498 ticket directories carry material referencing the OLD env id, 7 carry both,\n4 carry only the new one.\n\n`rebar review-plan <id> --status` on task b2bb then reported:\n    verdict: foreign_key — \"op-cert signed by a different environment\n    (principal 'ade0f2ef-…'; this environment is 'b8a354e0-…')\"\nso the claim gate demanded a fresh LLM review for an attestation that had already been earned.\n\n## Why 'just accept both env ids' does NOT fix it\n`_opcert_signing.verify_opcert_record` short-circuits on `principal != own_env_id`, but that\nis not the binding constraint. The step behind it verifies against\n`_opcert_own_public_key(tracker)` — the LOCAL `.pub`. Verifying a signature made by\n`ade0f2ef` needs THAT environment's public key, which is gone: the key files are git-ignored,\nwere never committed (`git log --all -- .opcert-key .opcert-key.pub .env-id` is empty), and\nthe old tracker directory was replaced with no backup on disk.\n\nSo an accept-list would move the failure from `foreign_key` to `mismatch`. Making it PASS\nwould require accepting the principal WITHOUT verifying the signature, which turns the\nattestation from a cryptographic claim into an honor-system label and defeats the gate.\n\n## Measured blast radius — small, which is why no data surgery is warranted\nAll 93 open tickets are in `plan_review_phase: planning`, so none advanced on the strength of\nan old attestation; they need a review before claiming regardless. The loss only costs a\nre-review for a ticket that had ALREADY earned a passing attestation but was not yet claimed\n(b2bb was one; it has been re-earned). Do NOT rewrite ticket history for this.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] Initializing/attaching to a tracker that ALREADY contains events does not silently mint\n      a new `.env-id`. It refuses, or warns loudly and names the consequence (every existing\n      attestation becomes foreign), with an explicit flag to proceed.\n- [ ] The re-clone/bootstrap path is documented with the exact local state that must be\n      carried over (`.env-id`, `.opcert-key`, `.opcert-key.pub`, `.ensure-applied`) and what\n      breaks per file if it is not.\n- [ ] `rebar doctor` (or fsck) reports when the store contains events stamped with an env id\n      other than the current one, so a silent re-identification is DETECTED rather than\n      discovered later at a gate.\n- [ ] Held-out test: a tracker with existing events + a fresh `.env-id` is reported by the\n      detector above and is not silently accepted.\n\n## Follow-on worth considering separately (the principled 'multiple env ids')\nPublish each environment's op-cert PUBLIC key into the store, the way author identities\nalready are, so an attestation from a PREVIOUSLY-TRUSTED environment stays verifiable in any\nclone and a re-clone is survivable. That is a real trust-model change — local\nsame-environment certification becomes a small federated trust root — and needs its own\ndesign and security review. It is the only form of 'accept multiple env ids' that is backed\nby a key you can actually check.\n\n## Provenance\nFound when the claim gate blocked task b2bb during the store/signing remediation session.","id":"eca3-d294-42f7-44b4","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Tracker re-clone silently re-identifies the environment, voiding attestations"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786383465942498001,"uuid":"b5935db2-d357-47e8-ba6b-46aff4622115"}